                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Sprinting around sprucing up a traditional RPG dungeon is the objective that [b]The Caretaker - Dungeon Nightshift[/b] offers you, much in the spirit of other post-carnage housekeeping simulators such as [i]Viscera Cleanup Detail[/i]. You've got garbage to throw in the incinerator (the game's insensitive term for the remains of murdered adventurers), treasure chests to restock and spike traps to reset, all before the sun comes up and another day of bowel-rupturing terror commences. \n\nHowever, the stringent time limit is the least of your worries here as you work against both a noise meter, easily raised by a combination of constant sprinting and the howling of the resident monsters that you must keep quiet with regular feedings, and the giant spiders that crawl along the dungeon ceiling who will insta-kill you should you make the mistake of jogging under them.\n\n[b]The Caretaker[/b] is simplistic in its design, throwing you levels in multiples of three that start with one area of the dungeon before expanding it twice more and then moving you onto a new map. The pick-this-up, stock-this, feed-this routine would be in danger of becoming fatally stale were it not for the challenging difficulty and the randomised placement of the chests and rubbish with each restart of the level. You can only hold two items at any one time, forcing you to make the most out of every trip out from the central kitchen. \n\nMost of your deaths will come from charging around a blind corner into the path of one of the aggravating spiders or taking your eye off the noise of your constant sprinting just as a monster starts screaming bloody murder for its dinner but nothing here feels overwhelmingly cheap or badly implemented enough to scoff at the cheap asking price.\n\nOpening stages of the game in the video below.\n(Rated R for aggravated outbursts at spiders)\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iBmlsCLlR_o",
